Give every event above exactly one disposition:

- `escalate_event` — this does need doing, and leaving it deferred was wrong.
  Set the priority it should have had, which says where it goes from here:
  `nudge` wakes triage for it now, `background` leaves it for triage's next
  heartbeat, and `async` or `active` send it to its stream's conversation.
- `handle_event` — this needs work now and is self-contained enough to hand
  straight to a subagent, without waiting for the next triage.
- `archive_event` — this will never need action. Archiving retires the event: it
  drops out of the queue for good and survives only in its action record. Say why, because
  that reason is the last word on it.
- `keep_deferred` — still not worth acting on, and not yet safe to retire. Give
  the reason as it stands now, not a copy of the old one, and say what would
  change it.

Two more tools act on your own schedule rather than on events, and count toward
no event's disposition:

- `schedule_check_in` — be woken in so many minutes with a note to yourself, when
  something is expected sooner than the next scheduled pass.
- `set_heartbeat_interval` — for a while, run passes more (or less) often than the
  standing schedule; it lapses on its own.

Guidance:

- Read the history first. An event deferred three times with the same reasoning
  is telling you something: either it deserves handling, or nothing will ever
  make it matter and it should be archived. Repeating the deferral is the one
  answer the history has already ruled out.
- Age cuts both ways. A question from a person going unanswered for days gets
  worse; an automated notification going unread for days does not.
- Do not archive anything a person is waiting on, however old. Silence is a
  worse answer than a late one.
- Escalating is cheap and reversible. Archiving is not, so require yourself to
  be able to say why the event can never matter, not merely why it does not
  matter today.

Work through them, then stop — do not summarize what you did.
